WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.090
Heap memory requires clear ownership and cleanup responsibility.

2
00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:10.928
A dynamically allocated object should have an owner, a documented lifetime, and a predictable release path.

3
00:00:10.928 --> 00:00:18.660
Without those rules, the codebase can accumulate stale pointers, leaks, duplicated cleanup, and confusing error behavior.

4
00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:23.581
A use-after-free occurs when code accesses memory after it has been released.

5
00:00:23.581 --> 00:00:32.655
The pointer value may still be stored in a variable, structure, callback context, or cache, but the allocation no longer belongs to that code.

6
00:00:32.655 --> 00:00:42.177
Defensive cleanup often sets pointers to null after release when reuse is possible, but the deeper fix is clear ownership and avoiding stale aliases.

7
00:00:42.177 --> 00:00:47.672
A double-free occurs when code attempts to release the same allocation more than once.

8
00:00:47.672 --> 00:00:58.536
This can happen when two components believe they own the same pointer, when an error path falls through into shared cleanup, or when ownership transfer is not documented.

9
00:00:58.536 --> 00:01:04.735
Cleanup should be centralized where practical and state should make repeated release less likely.

10
00:01:04.735 --> 00:01:09.463
Memory leaks happen when ownership is lost and cleanup becomes impossible.

11
00:01:09.463 --> 00:01:19.816
Leaks may not corrupt memory immediately, but they can degrade reliability over time, exhaust resources, and obscure which component is responsible for an object.

12
00:01:19.816 --> 00:01:27.420
Error paths are especially important because allocations often happen before all later operations are known to succeed.

13
00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:30.232
The realloc function needs careful handling.

14
00:01:30.232 --> 00:01:35.408
If it fails, the original allocation remains valid and still needs to be managed.

15
00:01:35.408 --> 00:01:41.543
Assigning the result directly over the only pointer can lose the original allocation on failure.

16
00:01:41.543 --> 00:01:48.509
A safer pattern uses a temporary pointer, checks the result, and updates the buffer state only after success.

17
00:01:48.509 --> 00:01:52.535
Cleanup paths should leave program state predictable and valid.

18
00:01:52.535 --> 00:01:54.963
On success, ownership should be clear.

19
00:01:54.963 --> 00:02:01.609
On failure, partially initialized objects should be either cleaned up or returned in a documented state.

20
00:02:01.609 --> 00:02:09.341
The next caller or cleanup routine should not have to guess which pointers are live and which have already been released.
